## 3. The reasoning behind the weights

The agency is measured on submitted applications and the cost per submitted application. Those costs differ by a factor of two and a half.

| Line | August cost per submitted application | Detail |
|---|---:|---|
| **Bing** | **$379** | 41 applications on $15,543 through August 26 |
| **Azerion** | **$978** | 25 applications through August 17; about 44 projected for the month |
| Quantcast | Not reported | Reports results, not submitted applications |
| Native | None we can trace to it | Delivers site visits only |

Each channel is read on the signal its own platform reports. The two application costs are not combined, and no cross-channel acquisition cost is shown, because the methods are not directly comparable.

### Azerion has become more expensive every month since April

| Month | Spend | Applications | Cost per application |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| April | $11,236 | 19 | $591 |
| May | $22,158 | 31 | $715 |
| June | $28,002 | 32 | $875 |
| July | $37,509 | 41 | $915 |
| August | $42,956 | ~44 | **$978** |

Spend nearly quadrupled and the cost of each application rose at every step. More money bought more applications, but each one cost more than the last. This is what a channel looks like once it has reached the people it could reach cheaply and has to pay more for each additional one. It is why Azerion is reduced in both options rather than protected. August is a month-end projection with its application count derived from the $978 recorded across August 1 to 17.

Bing moved the other way. The rebuilt account ran at about $237 per application in its first ten days after the July 22 relaunch and settled at $379 in August as volume grew. It costs less than half of Azerion.

Native, Azerion and Quantcast are booked under agreements we can cancel on 24 hours' notice, with no obligation to make up any delivery we do not take. Any of these reductions can be made quickly.

