## 4. Controlled Pullback, $61,000

Sets Bing at $25,000, below its approved $30,000 and above the $18,533 it is currently running, so the strongest channel gains weight while still costing less than the plan allows.

Cuts Azerion from $42,956 to $25,000, a 42% reduction, while keeping the line running, because it does produce applications at a cost we can measure.

Shuts off an out-of-date Quantcast campaign that is spending about $9,451 a month and produced nothing in August, and keeps about half of the rest running so we can watch whether it earns its place.

Removes Native, which produces no applications we can trace back to it.

This is the recommendation. It takes half the budget out while holding application volume close to flat, and it leaves the program able to ramp in October without rebuilding from zero.

---

## 5. Deep Pullback, $33,500

Holds Bing at its current pace, cuts Azerion to $15,000, and removes Quantcast and Native entirely. Only the two channels that produce submitted applications survive.

It preserves the most cash and adds spend nowhere. The cost is volume: roughly 64 applications a month against 93 today. It also leaves no audience built up in the display channels, which makes the ramp described below slower and more expensive.

**Where the two differ.** Both keep the channels that convert and remove the one that does not. They differ on weight, and on whether keeping a presence in the display channels is worth $11,000 a month. Quantcast is the weakest line in the program on the measure that counts: it reports results rather than submitted applications, most of those results are credited to people who saw an ad rather than clicked one, and the portion aimed at people who have already visited the site is both small and expensive. It survives in the Controlled Pullback as a line we watch rather than one we trust, kept because rebuilding a display audience takes three to four weeks. If it cannot show by the October decision that its results are real, it should go to zero there too.

